<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="hypusine biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5905" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The eukaryotic translation initiation factor 5A (eIF-5A), which is present in all eukaryotic cells, must be activated by post translational modification before it can participate in the initiation of protein synthesis. The modification involves the addition of a |FRAME: BUTYLAMINE| residue (derived from |FRAME: SPERMIDINE|) to a highly conserved lysine in the eIF-5A protein |CITS: [11278418]|. This results in the formation of an unusual amino acid called |FRAME: CPD-9974|. The biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-9974| occurs exclusively within the eIF-5A precursor, and is catalyzed by two enzymatic steps, |FRAME: MONOMER-13914| (DHS) and |FRAME: HS05318-MONOMER| (DOHH) |CITS: [10542236]|. The biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-9974| is found in all eukaryotes and the DHS and DOHH enzymes are highly conserved, indicating that the fundamental cellular function of elF5A is maintained from plants to mammals. DHS enzymes have been isolated and characterized from several animals and plants. The second enzyme (DOHH) has been charaterized from mammals, and homologs were identified in the |FRAME: TAX-3701| and |FRAME: TAX-4527| genomes. In tomato there is an increase in the abundance of DHS mRNA as well as elf5A mRNA in senescing flowers and fruits and in environmentally stressed leaves, suggesting that this enzyme plays a role in programmed cell death |CITS: [11278418]|. The antisense suppression of deoxyhypusine synthase in tomato dealys fruit softening and alters growth and development |CITS: [15951486]|. The strict substrate specificity for hypusine modification provides possible drug targeting of these enzymes, particularly in aberrant cell proliferation |CITS: [17476569]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="O-27" />
    <Author name="pujar" />
  </Authors>
